{
  "sections": {
    "borrowed": {
      "type": "custom-advertorial-kapoor",
      "blocks": {
        "lede_open": {
          "type": "lede",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Three coffees a day is not a caffeine habit. It is a repayment schedule. The first one covers a deficit from last night, the second covers the cost of the first, and by the third you are paying interest on a debt you never consciously took out.</p><p>Nothing in that sentence is a criticism of coffee. It is a description of what a stimulant does, and of why the dose always drifts upward.</p>",
            "align": "left"
          }
        },
        "head_mechanism": {
          "type": "section_head",
          "settings": {
            "label": "01 · The mechanism",
            "title": "Caffeine doesn’t add energy. It blocks the message about not having it.",
            "align": "center",
            "small": false,
            "tight": false
          }
        },
        "lede_mechanism": {
          "type": "lede",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Adenosine is the molecule your brain uses to keep score. It accumulates through the day as cells spend energy, and as it builds it makes you feel progressively more tired. That is not a fault. It is an accurate report on how much you have used.</p><p>Caffeine works by sitting in the adenosine receptor without activating it. The adenosine is still there, still accumulating; you simply stop receiving the message. Nothing has been added to the account - the statement has been intercepted. When the caffeine clears, the full backlog lands at once, which is the three o’clock crash people describe as needing another coffee.</p><p>Sugar is the same trade on a shorter clock. It raises available glucose sharply, insulin corrects the spike, and you finish lower than you started. Both of these are loans. Neither is a source.</p>",
            "align": "left"
          }
        },
        "mech1": {
          "type": "card_row",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "",
            "columns": "3",
            "label": "The signal",
            "title": "Adenosine builds as your cells spend. Tiredness is the readout.",
            "text": "",
            "dark": false
          }
