        "head_source": {
          "type": "section_head",
          "settings": {
            "label": "02 · The source",
            "title": "Real energy is manufactured, one cell at a time.",
            "align": "center",
            "small": false,
            "tight": false
          }
        },
        "lede_source": {
          "type": "lede",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Every usable unit of energy in your body is made by mitochondria, converting food and oxygen into ATP. It is a production line, not a reservoir - you hold only seconds of ATP at any moment, and everything you do depends on the rate at which it is remade.</p><p>That line runs on NAD⁺. It is the coenzyme that carries electrons through energy production, and the same molecule repair enzymes need to fix damage. Two systems, one shared pool, both drawing hardest overnight.</p><p>NAD⁺ declines steadily with age. By your forties you are running the same line on roughly half the supply you had in your twenties. Nothing feels broken - output is simply lower, and the gap between what you produce and what your day demands is exactly the gap a stimulant gets recruited to hide.</p>",
            "align": "left"
          }
        },
