        "head_cost": {
          "type": "section_head",
          "settings": {
            "label": "03 · The compounding cost",
            "title": "The loan is repaid out of the night, which is when production happens.",
            "align": "center",
            "small": false,
            "tight": false
          }
        },
        "lede_cost": {
          "type": "lede",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Caffeine has a half-life of around five hours, so a four o’clock coffee still has a meaningful dose circulating at bedtime. You may fall asleep normally and still spend less of the night in the deep, restorative stages, because the adenosine signal that drives sleep pressure was blunted rather than resolved.</p><p>That matters because the night is when the production line does its maintenance. Repair enzymes work through the queue, damage gets cleared, and the pool is restocked. Shorten that window and you wake with less capacity, which makes the morning harder, which makes the first coffee earlier and larger.</p><p>This is the loop, and it is not a discipline problem. It is arithmetic. Borrowed energy is repaid out of the same night you need for producing real energy.</p>",
            "align": "left"
          }
        },
